<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="ODE-solutions-complete">
    <title>Solution Methods to ODEs (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        While solving ODEs is a complex task, there are two classes of ODEs that are particularly important
                        for us. We'll outline these specific cases and how to approach them so we can use their solutions
                        in the near future.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Separable ODEs</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                In calculus 1, you may have seen problems like
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dt} = \cos(t)
                </md>.
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        This is an example of a <term>separable ODE</term>. The general form of a separable ODE is
                        <md>
                            \frac{dy}{dt} = f(t)g(y)
                        </md>,
                        and we can attempt to solve them by re-writing the equation as
                        <md>
                            \int \frac{1}{g(y)}dy = \int f(t) dt
                        </md>.
                        If we can solve both integrals, then we have an (implicit) description of the solution function <m>y</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Examples: Separable Equations</title>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\psi'(t) = 6 \psi(t)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \frac{1}{\psi} d\psi \amp= \int 6 dt </mrow>
                        <mrow>\ln|\psi| \amp= 6t + C  </mrow>
                        <mrow>\psi(t)  \amp=Ae^{6t}, A \neq 0 </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x'(t) = \frac{t}{x}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x dx \amp= \int t dt </mrow>
                        <mrow>0.5x^2 \amp=0.5t^2 + C </mrow>
                        <mrow>x^2 \amp= t^2 + 2C </mrow>
                        <mrow>x(t)  \amp= \pm\sqrt{t^2 + 2C} </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Note that an initial condition is needed to determine <m>C</m>, as well as whether we use the positive 
                    or negative version of the solution. See <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/jeygatmoym">Desmos</url> for a visual.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Second-Order Linear with Constant Coefficients</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A <term> homogeneous second-order linear equation with constant coeefficients</term> has the form
                <md>
                    y''(t) + by'(t) + cy(t) =0
                </md>.
                Solutions to this ODE are found by determining the roots of the <term>characteristic polynomial</term>:
                <md>
                    r^2 + br + c = 0
                </md>.
                If <m>r</m> is a root of this quadratic, then <m>y(t) = e^{r t}</m> is a solution to 
                the DE. There are 3 cases to form a general solution:
                
            </p>
           
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Two distinct real roots <m>r_1, r_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>y(t) = A e^{r_1 t} + B e^{r_2 t}</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Two complex conjugate roots, <m>r_1=a +bi</m>, <m>r_2 = a -bi</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>y(t) =A e^{(a+bi)t} + B e^{(a-bi) t} </m>. Euler's formula can be used to obtain a real-valued
                    general solution 
                    <md>
                        y(t) = Ae^{at} \cos(bt) + B e^{at}\sin(bt)
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    One repeated real root <m>r</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    In this case, <m>te^{r t}</m> is also a solution, so the general solution is
                    <md>
                        y(t) Ae^{r t} + Bte^{r t}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise workspace="10in">
        <title>Example: Solving a Second Order Initial Value Problem</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve <md>y'' + 16y=0, y(\pi/2)=-10, y'(\pi/2) = 3</md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The associated characteristic polynomial is <m>\lambda^2 + 16=0</m>, which has roots <m>\lambda = \pm 4i</m>. 
                So the general solution is 
                <md>
                    y(t) = A \cos(4t) + B\sin(4t)
                </md>.
                Using the initial data, we can solve for <m>A = -10</m>, <m>B = 0.75</m>.
                
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>
</page>



       
    </worksheet>
